{"qid":"Q917","names":["Kingdom of Bhutan"],"title":"Kingdom of Bhutan","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Kingdom of Bhutan traces its political formation to the Zhabdrung Ngawang Namgyal, a Drukpa Kagyu lama who fled Tibet in 1616 and unified the Himalayan valleys under a dual monastic-civic governance system, establishing a theocratic state. After a period of internal succession struggles following the Zhabdrung's death (1651), Bhutan was governed by a series of administrative regents (Desi) until Ugyen Wangchuck consolidated power and was crowned the first hereditary Druk Gyalpo (Dragon King) on 17 December 1907. Under a 1910 treaty Bhutan accepted British guidance in foreign affairs while retaining internal sovereignty; it became fully independent in 1947 and transitioned from an absolute to a constitutional monarchy in 2008.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Bhutan's core territory is well attested; exact boundaries with Tibet to the north have historically been disputed and were only partially delimited, while the southern border with India was defined by British surveys.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Bhutan","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bhutan"},{"label":"Britannica: Bhutan","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Bhutan"},{"label":"Britannica: Zhabdrung Ngawang Namgyal","url":"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Zhabdrung-Ngawang-Namgyal"}],"britannica":"place/Bhutan","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Bhutan","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Ngawang Namgyal, 1st Zhabdrung Rinpoche","nativeName":"ངག་དབང་རྣམ་རྒྱལ","roman":"Ngawang Namgyal","start":1616,"end":1651,"image":null,"imageUrl